  <main id="main-content" role="main">
    <article id="poster" aria-label="A0 academic poster on sclerosponge thermometry and global warming exceeding 1.5 degrees Celsius">
      <div class="poster-inner">
        <section class="poster-header" aria-labelledby="poster-title">
          <div class="topbar">
            <div class="eyebrow">Marine proxy thermometry · 1700–present</div>
            <div class="venue">Designed for AGU / Nature Climate Change Symposium<br>Academic conference poster</div>
          </div>
          <div class="header-grid">
            <div>
              <h1 id="poster-title">300 Years of Sclerosponge Thermometry Shows Global Warming Has <span class="headline-emphasis">Exceeded 1.5 °C</span></h1>
              <p class="subtitle">Sr/Ca records preserved in long-lived Caribbean sclerosponges extend the temperature baseline back to ~1700 CE, revealing that the conventional 1850–1900 reference period starts after substantial warming had already occurred.</p>
            </div>
            <aside class="hero-callout" aria-label="Hidden warming summary">
              <div class="kicker">Central finding</div>
              <div class="big-number">+0.5<sup>°C</sup></div>
              <p class="callout-text">of warming is hidden before the instrumental baseline, shifting total warming from ~1.2 °C to ~1.7 °C relative to true pre-industrial conditions.</p>
              <div class="comparison">
                <div class="box ipcc"><div class="label">IPCC baseline</div><div class="value">~1.2 °C</div></div>
                <div class="box true"><div class="label">1700–1790 baseline</div><div class="value">~1.7 °C</div></div>
              </div>
            </aside>
          </div>
        </section>
        <section class="metrics-strip" aria-label="Key metrics">
          <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">Record Length</div><div class="metric-value">~300 years<br>back to ~1700 CE</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">Hidden Warming</div><div class="metric-value">~0.5 °C already present by 1850–1900</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">True Total Warming</div><div class="metric-value">&gt;1.5 °C exceeded by early 2020s</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">IPCC-Reported</div><div class="metric-value">~1.2 °C as of 2020</div></div>
          <div class="metric"><div class="metric-label">Proxy Source</div><div class="metric-value">Sr/Ca in Caribbean sclerosponge skeletons</div></div>
        </section>
        <section class="main-grid" aria-label="Poster main content">
          <section class="card chart-card" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
            <div class="section-title">
              <h2 id="timeline-title">300-Year Temperature Reconstruction</h2>
              <div class="note">Temperature anomaly relative to a true pre-industrial 1700–1790 baseline; threshold crossing highlighted.</div>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-wrap">
              <canvas id="temperatureTimeline" role="img" aria-label="Line chart showing reconstructed warming from near zero in 1700 to about 1.7 degrees Celsius by 2020, crossing the 1.5 degree threshold in the early 2020s"></canvas>
            </div>
            <div class="chart-annotations">
              <div class="annotation"><strong>1700–1790: true baseline</strong><span>Stable pre-industrial temperatures define the reference period before broad instrumental coverage.</span></div>
              <div class="annotation warm"><strong>1850–1900: already warm</strong><span>The conventional reference interval sits ~0.5 °C above the 1700–1790 mean.</span></div>
              <div class="annotation red"><strong>Early 2020s: threshold exceeded</strong><span>Total warming reaches ~1.7 °C, placing the Paris 1.5 °C guardrail behind us.</span></div>
            </div>
          </section>
          <aside class="side-grid" aria-label="Supporting sections">
            <section class="card mini-card" aria-labelledby="why-title">
              <h2 id="why-title">Why Sclerosponges?</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Long-lived aragonite skeletons provide continuous annual-scale records over centuries.</li>
                <li>Sr/Ca ratios track ambient seawater temperature in tropical Caribbean environments.</li>
                <li>The proxy is independent of instrumental networks that begin around 1850.</li>
              </ul>
            </section>
            <section class="card mini-card" aria-labelledby="baseline-title">
              <h2 id="baseline-title">Pre-Industrial Baseline Matters</h2>
              <p>Using 1850–1900 as “pre-industrial” omits early industrial warming. The baseline choice changes the headline warming estimate by roughly half a degree.</p>
              <div class="baseline-graphic" aria-label="Visual comparison of 1.2 degrees and 1.7 degrees warming">
                <div class="bar-label left">Cool proxy baseline</div><div class="bar-label right">Warming present day</div>
                <div class="baseline-marker ipcc"><div class="tag">~1.2 °C</div></div>
                <div class="baseline-marker true"><div class="tag">~1.7 °C</div></div>
                <div class="delta-badge">hidden +0.5 °C</div>
              </div>
            </section>
            <section class="card mini-card implication" aria-labelledby="implications-title">
              <h2 id="implications-title">Policy & Target Implications</h2>
              <div class="impact-box"><strong>1.5 °C carbon budget</strong><span>A sclerosponge-informed baseline reduces remaining budget for 1.5 °C to effectively zero.</span></div>
              <div class="impact-box"><strong>2.0 °C guardrail</strong><span>Remaining warming margin is closer to ~0.3 °C, not the ~0.8 °C implied by conventional accounting.</span></div>
              <div class="impact-box"><strong>Scientific communication</strong><span>Baseline definitions must be explicit because they alter perceived proximity to climate targets.</span></div>
            </section>
          </aside>
        </section>
        <section class="footer" aria-label="Methods and takeaway">
          <div class="card mini-card">
            <h2>Data & Methods</h2>
            <div class="method-grid">
              <div class="method"><div class="num">01 · Samples</div><div class="txt">Caribbean reef sclerosponges collected from 33–91 m depths.</div></div>
              <div class="method"><div class="num">02 · Calibration</div><div class="txt">Sr/Ca measured by ICP-OES; calibrated to co-located SST from 1960 onward (r² &gt; 0.9).</div></div>
              <div class="method"><div class="num">03 · Validation</div><div class="txt">Agreement with HadCRUT5 and NOAA ERSSTv5 over instrumental overlap; divergence emerges pre-1850.</div></div>
            </div>
            <p class="source"><span class="pill">Nature Climate Change 2024</span><span class="pill">HadCRUT5 / IPCC AR6 comparison</span><span class="pill">NOAA ERSSTv5 validation</span></p>
          </div>
          <div class="takeaway">The Paris threshold is not only a future risk: with a <span>true pre-industrial baseline</span>, 1.5 °C has already been surpassed.</div>
        </section>
      </div>
